        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Sekigahara</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">21 October 1600 (Keichō 5, 15th day of the 9th month); the start is put at about 08:00 by tradition and closer to 10:00 by recent Japanese research</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Sekigahara, a narrow basin between Mount Nangu and Mount Matsuo, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu">Gifu</a> Prefecture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Eastern Army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa">Ii Naomasa</a> as supreme field commander, against the Western Army, a coalition formally led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a> and led in the field by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> on behalf of the child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> Hideyori. Ii's unit numbered 3,600 and the defecting Kobayakawa forces over 15,000.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Eastern Army won after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Kobayakawa Hideaki</a> and other Western commanders changed sides. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Mitsunari</a> was captured and executed on 6 November, and about 87 daimyō lost their domains.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="empire-meta">6 min read</p>
      </div>
