    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Western Army's commanders were divided, and several of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Kobayakawa Hideaki</a> among them, defected before or during the fighting, so the battle was settled by loyalties as much as by arms.</li><li>Recent Japanese research puts the battle at about two hours, from 10:00 to 12:00, not the full day of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> accounts, and doubts the story that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> fired on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Hideaki</a> to force him to act.</li><li>The victory gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> the authority to redistribute domains worth 6.8 million koku, and in 1603 he was appointed shōgun.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>'s own letter of 21 October 1600 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a>, the primary source for the duration, led the historian Andō Yūichirō to estimate that the battle took only two hours, from 10:00 to 12:00.</p></aside>
    </section>

