    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1598</span><span class="rf-text">Daimyō of the Military Faction march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>'s residence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> mediates</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1600</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> asks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kagekatsu">Uesugi Kagekatsu</a> to explain his military build-up, and Kagekatsu refuses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1600</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Mitsunari</a>, Ōtani Yoshitsugu, Ankokuji Ekei and Mashita Nagamori agree to ask <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a> to command; he reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> on 22 August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1600</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Mitsunari</a>'s Western Army arrives at Ōgaki Castle on 15 September</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1600</span><span class="rf-text">The two coalitions make contact at Kuisegawa on 20 October, and both move to Sekigahara</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1600</span><span class="rf-text">The battle of 21 October is fought; Western commanders defect and the Western Army collapses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1600</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Mitsunari</a>, Konishi Yukinaga and Ankokuji Ekei are captured and executed on 6 November</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1603</span><span class="rf-text">Emperor Go-Yōzei appoints <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> shōgun</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari.jpg" alt="Ishida Mitsunari" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ishida_Mitsunari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>'s heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> Hideyori, was only five years old at his death, and the result was a power vacuum. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imjin-war">Imjin War</a> factions formed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> and seven former Toyotomi generals including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-kiyomasa">Katō Kiyomasa</a>. The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-toshiie">Maeda Toshiie</a>, a long-term ally of Hideyoshi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> who had been sympathetic to Mitsunari, loosened the balance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> drew Kiyomasa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori">Fukushima Masanori</a> to his side, while Mitsunari claimed to act for the Toyotomi. In 1598 several daimyō of the Military Faction marched on Mitsunari's residence, and Ieyasu mediated.</p><p>Tensions rose in 1599 over rumoured plots against Ieyasu. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kagekatsu">Uesugi Kagekatsu</a>, one of Hideyori's regents, built up his army, and when Ieyasu demanded an explanation, Naoe Kanetsugu answered with a mocking letter pointing to Ieyasu's own violations of Hideyoshi's orders. In May 1600 Kagekatsu refused to explain himself, and Ieyasu called his allies to punish the Uesugi.</p><p>Mitsunari met Ōtani Yoshitsugu, Mashita Nagamori and Ankokuji Ekei to plan an anti-Tokugawa army, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a> as overall commander. This coalition came to be called the Western Army, and Terumoto captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> while the main Tokugawa army was marching to suppress Kagekatsu. The Tokugawa-led coalition was the Eastern Army.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Sekigahara&#8221; cites where it covers Background:</p><ul><li>Anthony J. Bryant, Sekigahara 1600: The final struggle for power (Bloomsbury Publishing, 1995)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Ieyasu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokugawa_Ieyasu2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kanō Tan'yū, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> left Edo on 7 October with 30,000 men, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa">Ii Naomasa</a>'s unit, the Ii no Akazonae, numbered 3,600 crimson-clad troops. Ieyasu had bought large quantities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanegashima">Tanegashima</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a>, and Spanish accounts record that 19 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ship De Liefde were used by the Tokugawa army as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">field artillery</a>. Large parts of both armies took no part: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Tokugawa Hidetada</a> and Sakakibara Yasumasa, with as many as 38,000 soldiers, were held at the Siege of Ueda by the Sanada, and 15,000 Western soldiers were delayed at Tanabe by 500 troops under Hosokawa Yūsai.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Mitsunari</a> had meant to use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu-castle">Gifu Castle</a>, held by Oda Hidenobu, and Ōgaki Castle as choke points, but Gifu Castle was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori">Fukushima Masanori</a> and Ii Naomasa before the main Western forces arrived. Learning that the Tokugawa army was moving towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>, he prepared to fight in the open at Sekigahara.</p><p>The field is a narrow basin with Mount Nangu to the south and Mount Matsuo to the north. Stephen Turnbull notes that it forced the Western Army into a linear formation, limiting its numerical advantage, and gave defectors high ground from which to enfilade its lines. Jakob Meckel, a Prussian general who advised Meiji <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, read a map of the forces and judged that the Western Army, with greater numbers and higher ground, should have won, and was at first sceptical of the Japanese officer who explained how the Eastern Army had.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki.jpg" alt="Kobayakawa Hideaki" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Kobayakawa Hideaki</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kobayakawa_Hideaki_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At dawn on 21 October the Tokugawa advance guard stumbled into Ishida's army in dense fog, and both sides panicked and withdrew, though each now knew where the other stood. Tradition puts the start at about 08:00, but recent Japanese research puts it closer to 10:00. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa">Ii Naomasa</a>, appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> supreme field commander, attacked the Western centre with his 3,600 men, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori">Fukushima Masanori</a> followed and engaged Ukita Hideie. The battle then reached a deadlock, which the eyewitness Ōta Gyūichi described as friends and foes pushing each other under thundering gunfire and hails of arrows.</p><p>The Western Army then began to fray. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Kobayakawa Hideaki</a>, who had agreed beforehand to change sides, attacked Ōtani Yoshitsugu from behind with over 15,000 men according to one contemporary record, and the commanders Wakisaka Yasuharu, Ogawa Suketada, Akaza Naoyasu and Kutsuki Mototsuna also changed sides. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a>'s force stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>, and Kikkawa Hiroie, who had colluded with the Eastern Army, held up the Mōri troops in front of Mōri Hidemoto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-morichika">Chōsokabe Morichika</a>.</p><p>Ukita Hideie's front gave way, Yoshitsugu killed himself, and Shima Sakon was fatally shot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-yoshihiro">Shimazu Yoshihiro</a>'s troops were surrounded and broke out after devastating casualties with only 200 soldiers left, using a rearguard action later called sutegamari. Andō Yūichirō estimates that the whole battle lasted two hours, from 10:00 to 12:00, and Ieyasu's own letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> supports him.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Sekigahara&#8221; cites where it covers Feuding factions, Battle, Western Army defectors, Collapse of the Western Army:</p><ul><li>Anthony J. Bryant, Sekigahara 1600: The final struggle for power (Bloomsbury Publishing, 1995)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto.jpg" alt="Mōri Terumoto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mori_Terumoto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> feared that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a>, with troops still largely intact, would hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> with Hideyori, and persuaded him to leave by promising a peaceful settlement. He then went back on his promise: he gave the Mōri lands to Kikkawa Hiroie, citing seized letters that implied Terumoto's major role in the anti-Tokugawa movement. Hiroie refused and petitioned for Terumoto, and Ieyasu spared the clan but cut its holdings from eight provinces to two, Suō and Nagato, worth 298,000 koku. Ōgaki Castle surrendered when news arrived, and its garrison commander was pardoned.</p><p>Ieyasu redistributed domains worth 6.8 million koku, mostly to the allies who had helped him, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-terumasa">Ikeda Terumasa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori">Fukushima Masanori</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-kiyomasa">Katō Kiyomasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Kobayakawa Hideaki</a>, who received 520,000 koku. Roughly 87 daimyō lost their domains for supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Mitsunari</a>. The Chōsokabe lost Tosa, where a revolt of former retainers was put down in five weeks with about 273 killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-yoshihiro">Shimazu Yoshihiro</a>'s clan negotiated until 1602 and was the only Western Army clan to lose no territory.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawayama-castle">Sawayama Castle</a>, Mitsunari's home, fell, and most of his relatives were killed or died by their own hands. On 6 November Mitsunari, Konishi Yukinaga and Ankokuji Ekei were captured and executed. Ieyasu had a burial mound for heads, a Kubizuka, built for those who had fallen. In 1603 Emperor Go-Yōzei appointed him shōgun.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Sekigahara&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Constantine Nomikos Vaporis, Samurai: an encyclopedia of Japan's cultured warriors (ABC-CLIO, 2019)</li><li>Anthony J. Bryant, Sekigahara 1600: The final struggle for power (Bloomsbury Publishing, 1995)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most of what is known comes from Japanese chronicles and letters, and from historians who read them. Ōta Gyūichi, who was present, wrote a chronicle, and Nakatomi Yūhan-ki records the Kobayakawa attack. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>'s own letter of 21 October to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> is a primary source for the timing, and Spanish accounts record the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. The Keichō Kenbunsho preserves Ōtani Yoshitsugu's warning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Mitsunari</a> that he was hated by the daimyō. Hayashi Gahō, writing in 1664 as a Tokugawa historian, praised Ieyasu in terms of martial virtue and the peace he founded, so that later accounts of the battle rest on pro-Tokugawa tradition.</p><p>Historians dispute the start (08:00 or 10:00), the length (two hours, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> view of twice that) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Hideaki</a>'s defection. The older tradition has him hesitate until about noon and shows Ieyasu firing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebuses</a> at his position, a story from an Edo-period anecdote. Jun Shiramine and Junji Mitsunari hold that he had already changed sides before the battle, and Turnbull, Masayuki Fujimoto and Yūichi Goza doubt the shooting, which Goza suggests may be dramatisation by pro-Tokugawa historiography. The Shimazu breakout is also questioned: modern historians caution that the charge of Shimazu Toyohisa, later told as heroic, may be Edo-period embellishment. Some anecdotes, such as Komatsuhime's refusal to admit her father-in-law, may be partly apocryphal.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Mitsunari</a>'s defeat is generally considered the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> for two and a half centuries until 1868. The battle also shifted the authority to assign ranks and lands from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi clan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>. Watanabe Daimon suspects the verbal grants of domains reflect Ieyasu's wariness of Hideyori.</p><p>Historians explain the result politically. Kasaya Kazuhiko, Mizuno Goki, Watanabe Daimon and others point to the resentment many daimyō, especially veterans of the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, felt towards Mitsunari. Yūichi Goza concludes that Mitsunari overestimated the authority of the Toyotomi, while Toshi Kohara holds that the daimyō thought fighting for Ieyasu served Hideyori's interests best, and Kazuto Hongo calls the campaign more a political than a military victory for Ieyasu. Watanabe cautions that rumours of Mitsunari's slander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Hideaki</a> may reflect a bias that blamed him for everything that went wrong before the battle.</p><p>The battlefield was registered as a Monument of Japan in 1931, with the positions of Ieyasu and Mitsunari and the place of Ōtani Yoshitsugu's death marked. The battle appears in Ryōtarō Shiba's novel Sekigahara (1966) and its 2017 film, in James Clavell's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">Shōgun</a>, and in several video games and television dramas.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Sekigahara on 21 October 1600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>'s Eastern Army defeated the Western Army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> for the child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> Hideyori, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Kobayakawa Hideaki</a> and others changed sides. The victory gave Ieyasu the power to redistribute domains, and in 1603 he became shōgun.</em></p>

